---
import { getLiveCollection } from "astro:content";
import { LiveIcon } from "astro-icon/components";
import Base from "../layouts/base.astro";

// `listIcons()` on the custom source is what makes this possible: the page asks
// the backend what it holds instead of hardcoding names. A source that only
// implements `getIcon` still works with <LiveIcon>, it just can't be enumerated.
const { entries, error } = await getLiveCollection("brand");
---

<Base title="Brand kits">
  <h1>Icons from a custom source</h1>

  <p class="note">
    <strong>A backend astro-icon doesn't ship.</strong> The <code>brand</code>
    collection is served by <code>src/lib/brandKitSource.ts</code>, a custom
    <code>IconSource</code> - three methods (<code>name</code>,
    <code>getIcon</code>, and an optional <code>listIcons</code>). It stands in
    for a per-tenant brand kit: a design-tool export or a database table, where
    the icons aren't yours and can change without a deploy.
  </p>

  {error && <p class="error">Couldn't load the brand kit: {error.message}</p>}

  {entries && (
    <div class="grid">
      {entries.map((entry) => (
        <div class="cell">
          <LiveIcon collection="brand" icon={entry.id} size={32} />
          <code>{entry.id}</code>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  )}

  <p class="note">
    The source's <code>name</code> has to match the key it's registered under in
    <code>src/live.config.ts</code>. A <code>LiveLoader</code> is never told its
    own collection name, so typegen records the live collection under the
    source's name instead - if they drift,
    <code>&lt;LiveIcon collection="brand"&gt;</code> stops type-checking against
    a collection that appears not to exist.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .error {
    color: #b00;
  }
</style>
